# act configuration for local CI runs.
#
# `act` parses .github/workflows/build.yml and runs each job inside a Docker
# container that emulates a GitHub-hosted runner. Defaults below favour
# fidelity to actual CI over speed/disk — the trade-off most people miss is
# that "small image + apt-install everything" *seems* faithful but skips
# nuances of the GHA runner image (timezone defaults, locale, default tools,
# ImageMagick / fonts being preinstalled, sudoers tweaks, etc.).

# Map ubuntu-24.04 to catthehacker's `full-24.04` image. ~17GB but mirrors
# what `runs-on: ubuntu-24.04` actually launches: node, Python, Java, GH-CLI,
# Docker-in-Docker, az CLI, kubectl, gcloud, plus the ~500 packages GHA
# ships preinstalled. Our jobs only need a small subset, but anything that
# accidentally relies on a preinstalled tool will behave the same locally.
# (`act-24.04` is ~1.5GB and works for most jobs but skips a lot of this.)
-P ubuntu-24.04=ghcr.io/catthehacker/ubuntu:full-24.04

# Force x86_64 even on systems where Docker might pick a different arch by
# default. Our CI is x86_64-only; matching that here means the same
# instruction set, the same apt repositories, the same compiler defaults.
--container-architecture linux/amd64

# Cap container resources to match GHA-hosted ubuntu-24.04: 4 vCPUs / 16GB
# RAM. Without this, parallel build/test steps will use as many cores as
# your host has, which silently masks timing-dependent issues — flaky TSan
# reports, CTest jobs that race differently, ccache thrash patterns. Match
# CI's constraints to reproduce CI's behaviour.
# NB: each --container-options line is a single Docker flag; .actrc splits
# on whitespace so multi-word values inside one quoted string don't survive.
--container-options --cpus=4
--container-options --memory=16g

# Persist `actions/cache@v5` content across `act` invocations. Without this,
# every run starts from a cold ccache, which makes iterating on a failing
# job painfully slow and hides "wait, why is this slow on CI but fast for
# me?" issues. The directory is bind-mounted into act's internal cache
# server; nothing else writes there.
--artifact-server-path /tmp/act-artifacts

# Reuse the runner container across `act` invocations. apt-installed
# packages and (with the cache server above) ccache state survive, so
# re-running `act -j build-no-zstd` is seconds instead of minutes.
# Use `act --rm` for a from-scratch run when you want to validate "does
# this still work on a clean GHA-hosted runner?" — that's the one case
# where cold-start fidelity matters.
--reuse

# Default to the build workflow only. Without `-W`, act would also pick up
# release.yml (tag-triggered) and try to drive its `softprops/action-gh-
# release@v3` step, which doesn't make sense locally.
-W .github/workflows/build.yml
